A||||11||86SE||Anyone ported before, or know about porting? I got some questions....||||5||
Z||000000||86SE||07-02-2000||05:58 PM||||We have all the equipment we need to port the upper and lower intake manifolds, throttle body and all that.  A went to a website that Oreif posted, and they showed the HP increase after they ported each thing.<P>Throttle Body - 4 HP<BR>Upper Intake - 6 HP<BR>Lower Intake - 8 HP<P>So if I get all these ported the motor should have about 158 HP +/- a few ponys.  Then if I port the heads they say thats another 25 HP to bring it up to about 190 HP!!  That seems CRAZY out of a 2.8!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>Has anyone ever ported these things before?  Was the power increase noticeable (faster 0-60, scores great on GT Bastards ass-o-meter, or decrease 1/4 mile times)???<P>How much and how deep did you open the air pleniums up? And, has anyone polished their intake manifolds to a nice shine??  Was there any HP increase after that??<P>One more question, does the HP increase with the further you widen the openings (wider opening=more HP)??<P>Thanks for any answers to any of my 200 questions!!  Im seriously thinking about porting my intake..... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||olympic||07-02-2000||06:08 PM|||| oops, forgot sig  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by olympic (edited 07-02-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000002||olympic||07-02-2000||06:08 PM||||  Just remember that all this porting wont make your car a tire smoking monster.  You will get lots of top end hp but little more low end torque(if not less).  The only way to significantly increase torque is to increase displacement or use forced induction.  <BR>    Since you already have the necessary equipment to do the porting I say go for it.<P>------------------<BR>"Born free....taxed to death."||||reg||3
Z||000003||86SE||07-02-2000||06:17 PM||||I probibly wont port the heads, seems like way too much work.  It would be nice to have the extra HP so I can say "Oh yeah?  My car has 160HP"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Anyway, are there any other ways of forced induction rater than a turbo and a super charger?  This probibly won't get you much force, but will taking out the air silencer, putting PCV tubing in its place and putting TOM's scoop on the side of it do anything for me??  I have a 'hi flow' K&N air filter, if that might make any differance......||||reg||1
Z||000004||hugh||07-02-2000||06:18 PM||||Are you going to polish the ports,or match them?Do you know how?I believe there is more benefit to just matching the ports with some polishing into the openings.Bigger is not always better,Olympic is right.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||86SE||07-02-2000||06:24 PM||||I was thinking about boring, then polishing to a very smooth finish.  I think I get what you are saying, I just polish the openings of all the ports and it will be more benificial, if so thats GREAT!!<P>What if I reverse this whole thing and DECREASE the size of the holes, shouldent that decrease high end and increase low end torque?||||reg||1
Z||000006||hugh||07-02-2000||06:30 PM||||Port matching is the most important.NO Decreasing would kill your engine power.<p>[This message has been edited by hugh (edited 07-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||86SE||07-02-2000||08:14 PM||||Do I bore the hole larger first, then polish?  Or do I just polish?  Is there any increase in power with it all polished up?||||reg||5
Z||000008||FIEROCIOUS||07-02-2000||08:22 PM||||Take a look at Standard Abrasives web site. I've been porting heads since High school and they have some good info. <P>As said matching the ports and cleaning up the pockets will be FAR more benificial than anything you could do to the intake or throttle body. <P>It's very important that you understand that bigger is not better and is usualy worse. Power increases should come from efficiency not large ports!<P>1. port the pocket and bowl section.<BR>2. remove casting errors<BR>3. smooth short side radius (do not remove too much material)<BR>4. smooth port cieling (high flow area)<BR>5. gasket match intake side (leave exhaust side alone (reversion)<BR>6. three angle valve job with valves back cut!<P>Remember DO NOT hog out the ports! Use the existing port design and make it more efficient by smoothing casting flaws!  <P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000009||Taijiguy||07-02-2000||10:00 PM||||This topic has been covered a number of times previously. One of the most primary thing to remember when porting the heads, is to leave the "shark fin" in the bottom of the pockets. It's there by design, and is beneficial to flow. It's OK to smooth them, but don't remove them. Also, it's practically impossible to port the upper plenum because of its shape. I ported the runners, but that's about as far as you can go. There is a place (I'll have to locate the website) that uses an abrasive paste that they pump through your item, (being heads, manifold, etc.)that smooths it out, and the neat thing is, that since it flows through the openings, it tends to port and polish following the same path that air would flow through. Pretty slick.<P>-Taiji<BR>modified 85 GT||||reg||1
Z||000010||88formula||07-02-2000||10:25 PM||||FIEROCIOUS is right. Don't hog out the ports or you will lose intake air velocity, which helps to ram the air into the combustion chamber. Try to remove as little material as possible. I did this to my brothers Iron Duke with the Standard Abrasives deluxe porting kit. I don't have numbers but the only difference in performance he says is that it pulls a little better at higher revolutions than before, its barely noticeable and if I were to do it to an Iron Duke again I wouldn’t do it. <P>I am in the process of doing this to my 88 formula heads and intake manifolds. I just got back as a matter of fact from porting my heads two hours ago. I have about twenty hours in them already including the intake manifolds. The exhaust manifolds took me about 2 ½ hours to do. I did not have to gasket match the upper intake to the middle intake manifolds, I just removed the casting flash. The lower intake was a lot of work matching it to the heads and the middle intake manifold. Make sure when you match them that you check them for fit by bolting them together. I had to torque the heads down with the head gaskets and the manifold gaskets in place to check the matching .I then looked down the ports with a flashlight and used a scribe to feel for the spots I couldn’t see.<P>Standard Abrasives web site and pictures <A HREF="http://www.sa-motorsports.com/diyguide.htm." TARGET=_blank>http://www.sa-motorsports.com/diyguide.htm.</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||olympic||07-03-2000||11:47 PM||||  The process Taijiguy is talking about is extude honing. It is a patented process and any part you want done has to be sent to this company. There site is <A HREF="http://www.extrudehone.com" TARGET=_blank>www.extrudehone.com</A>  <P>------------------<BR>"Born free....taxed to death."||||reg||1
